    "result_text": "Both parts of the change are in, and all 4 acceptance tests pass under `bin/e2e`.\n\n**Edit conflicts**\n- **Stored data:** a new migration, `V12__person_version.sql`, adds a `version` column to `person`. `Person` treats it as a JPA `@Version`, so every save moves it on.\n- **Server:** the person data the app sends now includes `version`. To edit a person, the request must include the version the editor loaded; without it the server answers 400. If someone else has saved the person since, `PersonService.edit` refuses with HTTP 412 and writes nothing to the store or the audit file. A save that loses a race inside the database (`ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException`) gets the same 412. A name clash still answers 409.\n- **Front end:** the people form remembers the version it opened. On a 412 it shows `person-form-error`, re-reads the people from the server, and fills the form with the current name and grade.\n\n**Double submit**\nThe person, claim and rate forms ignore a second submit while the first is still waiting for an answer, and accept submits again once it answers (success or error). That covers both adding and editing claims.\n\nTwo things you might not expect:\n- After a refused save, the form takes on the person's current version. Pressing Save again straight away therefore goes through without reopening the editor. The user is looking at the current values at that point, so I thought that was reasonable. If you'd rather force a fresh \"Edit\" click, it's a small change.\n- The double-submit guard is only in the front end; the server doesn't detect duplicate requests. I left out the sign-in, load-sheet and set-today forms, because submitting those twice doesn't store a second record.",
